ed to the task and  I told her I would be back before  sundown I waited at the banks of the  water legs crossed and pole in hand  it was a small bit of uncooked bacon at  the end of the line my concentration was  broken by the sound of music someone was  playing a fiddle the sound was  enchanting I looked around for the  source not finding one I tried to follow  the sound abandoned in the pole and the  bank with the line still in the water I  quietly crept along the bank walking  until I found the source of the music I  found who was playing the fiddle it was  a young man sitting on the branch of a  tree the branch hung just above the  water and the man laid against it  suspended over the mirror like surface  playing a tune - his wooden fiddle he  stopped when he saw me and smiled no  words came between us but he gestured to  move his hand to take a seat on the  mossy Bank and he continued to play the  music was wonderful when the song ended  I asked for him to play another he  nodded but only if I went further into  the water  my grandmother had been very clear with  me to keep out of the water I could not  swim at the time and she had made me  promise to stay on the bank he played  another song and when it finished he  gestured for me once again to come  closer even deeper into the water like  he was going to tell me a secret and  whisper it in my ear I shook my head I  had made a promise the young fiddler  seemed disappointed I can't quite  remember the details of his face but I  can remember he's frown he sighed and  rolled off the branch into the dark  water without a splash just a few small  ripples came from where he entered the  Bayou I never did see him leave the  water after that I went back to the  house as my grandmother called me first  I ran to the pole and saw a tiny fish at  the end of the paperclip hook  I almost told my grandmother about my  encounter with the young fiddler but I  chose not to she would just think it's  strange and say it was nonsense the next  day I went back to the Bayou banks  fishing pole in hand I said to my  grandmother that I will catch a bigger  fish I told her are to be back before  sundown  I went back to my spot and sat  cross-legged pole in hand there was a  small cut of deer on the hook i sat and  waited for the fish to bite my thoughts  trading off to my grandmother's stories  they were stopped by the sound of  laughter it was a girlish laughter light  and soft I was curious  usually the Bayou was so lonely just a  call from faraway birds in the chirp of  crickets I followed the sound leaving my  Pole on the bank and the line in the  water moving silently I walked along the  bank in the same place with the  low-hanging tree branch where I found  the source of the laughter that small  watery Grove seemed just a little  different a large gray rock sat in the  middle of the water  emerging from the deep I hadn't noticed  it before  possibly I just hadn't remembered it  from when I met the young fiddler  sitting on the rocks were three young  girls they looked a few years older than  me all of them had long dark hair that  swayed around them like thousands of  waves silk strings here in them laughs  made me happy I don't really know why I  got closer and sat in the bank and  watched them the girls were as beautiful  as the selkies in the tales my  grandmother told me they all had fair  skin that seemed to glow in the dimmed  by you light one of them met her dark  eyes of mine she gestured with a finger  towards me she wanted me to come and  play I wanted to they seemed as though  they were having so much fun up on the  rock I took off my shoes and rolled up  my pant legs I waded in up to my knees  and my feet sunk slightly into the mud  but looking down into the water I  remembered I couldn't swim  one by one they slid effortlessly into  the water and swam towards me only the  eyes are visible above the water with  their hair flowing behind them they swam  around my legs barely disturbing the  water one pulled gently at my leg  and another at my hand once again I  shook my head I could not play with them  disappointed they sighed dismal II and  let go of my hand and left slipping away  like the water they swam in I didn't  want them to go I almost swam in after  them but I heard my grandmother calling  my name I went to get my pole a small  fryer was at the end of the line I  almost told my grandmother about the  sake girls but I chose not to I felt  like they were mine somehow like a  secret that only I knew the following  day I set out again I was going to get a  bigger fish today  I had to this was my last day in the  Bayou I was going home the next day I  told my grandmother I'd be back before  sundown  and went to the bank to fish I gazed out  into the dark still water it seemed  almost dead lovely but dead a metallic  blue dragonfly landed on the water took  a sip and flew off  I watched it as it went my attention was  then turned to the most unusual noise  hooves and a neigh there were no horses  in the Bayou so I started to wonder I  put my paw down on the bank and left the  line to sit in the water  I followed the sounds of a brain horse  yet again I came to the same place the  willow hung low the tree branch sat  above the water and the rock was empty  of any selkie girls standing by the tree  on a small island bank in the middle of  the water was a unicorn it didn't have a  horn much to my disappointment but there  it was a pure white horse it poured at  the ground of its longford whose its  Mane was elegant and shiny it seemed to  glow just like the silky girl's skin and  the young man's fiddle strings it was  beautiful  even if it wasn't a unicorn as the Bayou  girls are not selkies it looked towards  me and waved his head up and down it was  calling me without hesitation  I got into the water I didn't even take  off my shoes I stood knee-deep the white  horse trotted into the water and began  to swim to me I was hoping it will play  with me on the banks or at least the  shallows it stopped though just a little  further out from where I was I couldn't  stand there but then again it was much  bigger than me I was sure the water  couldn't be that deep over there  it looked towards its back offering me a  riot in my excitement I forgot about my  grandmother's words and went deeper into  the water up to my chest  then my shoulders the water felt  suffocating as it went higher and higher  I felt my lungs were being crushed under  the pressure I held my hand to the white  horse it was still just out of reach I  took another step and the water was to  my chin my fingers brushed over its  silky main water weeds had collected in  the main giving it green flecks here and  there I went to touch it again this time  though it felt more sticky like tape or  glue looking down into the water the  white horse had lost its glow it seemed  more grey darkening the further it went  down until it was almost black maybe it  was just the water my foot slipped I  went down under the water opening my  eyes in panic I was horrified at what I  saw in front of me well the white horses  belly and legs would have been I only  saw smooth black decaying flesh and the  black legs fused together in a slowly  Fanning tail it was like something out  of a nightmare I immediately step back  my move been slowed by the water I  turned around and my head broke the  surface as I reached the shallows I  scrambled onto the bank and looks back  the white horse had gone I felt a relief  although I deeply missed the white horse  where had it gone I heard my grandmother  call my name and in my soaked muddy  clothes I ran back to my fishing pole a  large catfish was at the end of the hook  I left both of them where they were and  hurried back to my grandmother's house  I told my grandmother about the white  horse I did this time I left that the  selkie girls in the young Fiddler's for  my story and I did not mention the  nature of me falling into the water but  I asked about the white horse she told  me a tale about a kelpy it was a water  demon that often took the shape of a  beautiful white horse amongst other  things  such a handsome man playing a violin or  a young maiden it would offer a ride to  anyone willing then take them into the  water and drown them nothing would ever  be found of them that night I forced  myself to go back I needed to see if it  was real by the light of my torch I  followed the path I had taken as I  searched for the source of the sound  but off the hours of surgeon I couldn't  find it no green willows no tree branch  and no rock I went back home the next  day happy to be away he had desperate to  come back but I never did until recently  my grandmother had died a month before  she had only visited once since the last  time I spent the summer with her I  traveled back down to the bayou back to  her home to pack up her things and sell  the house I had almost forgotten most  three days down at the banks of the  Bayou the whole summer had been a blur  that year but going there brought the  memories back for so long I had  dismissed it as a dream or some dull  event of meat and other people a man  playing an instrument some girls  swimming in the water and an animal in  the bank a deer perhaps or white goat  that had lost its way nothing too out of  the ordinary for the south maybe the  white horse was just in my imagination  but to reassure myself of the childhood  nonsense I decided to go and just take  one little look that morning I'll be  back before sundown I found my old  fishing spot my pole was still there  somehow as if I had just left it I even  found the fresh carcass of the catfish  that I had left there years before I  tossed it into the water curious then I  heard music fiddle music and laughter  and the sound of a horse  I followed the sound and I found the  same place the place of the Willows in  the low-hanging tree branch and the rock  and the opposite bank 