ing up my  legs and chest I was running out of air  I grabbed the box color from my pocket  and cut the selkies decaying flesh it  screeched and looked at me with dead  eyes I saw my own reflection in them it  was angry it was in pain I slashed again  it bit me just inches from my face I had  managed to free my legs and as I  attempted to cut away the black flesh  around my arm and hand the kelpie jerked  and changed direction causing the box  cutter to dig deeply into my arm  silently screaming I watched in horror  as the loss of my heir escaped to the  surface I cut at the kelpie again and I  was free had a small glimpse I noticed  aloud at the bottom of the Bayou there  were bones down there human bones in  that short look I counted at least four  skulls the kelpie screamed and swam off  into the dark water as I pulled myself  to the surface I gasped and coughed as  my face was touched by the warm humid  Bayou air I looked around nothing was  moving dead silent I noticed a small  ripple a few meters away it got closer  and closer then it disappeared only a  second and passed before I felt  something grabbed my ankle and yanked me  back down under the water I was being  dragged back down the kelpie seemed  incessant that I would never make it  back to the banks I opened my eyes to  see myself face the face of the kelpie  its black mane flowed around me below me  the silky gills were grasping at my  ankles I jabbed the knife forward into  the kelpies eyes it screamed again such  an inhuman noise that he made my ears so  as if they were about to bleed I no  longer felt hands crossed open up my  legs the grip of my ankle was gone the  kelpie screaming swam away the  box-cutter still lodged in its I quickly  paddling my way to the bank I hope that  the kelpie would not come back for  revenge as I reached the shallows I  slowly walked forward holding my freely  bleeding arm blood dripped into the  water from my fingertips I crawled up  onto the mossy bank and laid on my back  for a moment to catch my breath  my legs were covered in mud up to my  knees blackening the ends of my rolled  up jeans I looked around  it was nearly night somehow the Sun was  gone and the first few stars began to  shine in the darkening sky the quiet and  beautiful Lagoon had changed in  appearance just like the creatures that  inhabited it the rock was mossy  crumbling and cracked the low-hanging  tree branch was broken and sticking out  of the water  all of the willow trees are dead their  leaves decane upon the ground in clumps  the rest of the trees look sickly as  well nothing here was healthy or alive  my hand touched something smooth looking  behind me I saw the remnants of a Polish  fiddle  it looked broken untouched for years  further away I saw the remnants of three  colorful beach towels they were just  threads now the skeletons of fish around  every discarded item looking closer into  the weeds I noticed more dozens of  things left behind by those who rode the  kelpie I never did go back to the Bayou  I sold my grandmother's house to a happy  family from out State New York I warned  them not to get too close to the waters  they might be Gators as I got into my  car and started to drive away I watched  as the boy talked his mother's sleeve  I'm going to the bayou  I'll just have a look I'll be back  before sundown my heart began to ache  for the boy's mother yes I told myself  I'm sure he'll be back before sundown
 

